---
<SiteLayout title={wd.title} activeSection="manual" activeSubject="air-to-ground-operations" compactSubjectNav={true}
 breadcrumbs={[{label:'Manual',href:sitePath('manual/')},{label:'Air-to-Ground Operations',href:sitePath('manual/air-to-ground-operations/')},{label:wd.title}]}>
 <section class="status-panel" data-module-id={wd.id} data-module-revision={wd.revision}>
  <h2>{wd.maturity}</h2>
  <p>Module revision {wd.revision} · How a fixed-wing pilot puts an unguided weapon on a target: the four kinds of attack, the geometry of a dive delivery, the numbers a pilot flies to and what an error in each does to the hit, and the offset <Term>pop-up</Term> attack from the run-in to the recovery. Written for military pilots of every service from the US Navy's T-45 weapons and operational navigation training instructions, with sim manuals for the computed modes.</p>
  <p>Figure 1 lets you fly a dive delivery badly on purpose and see where the bomb goes; Figure 2 steps through a <Term>pop-up</Term> attack in profile and plan.</p>
 </section>
 <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title"><h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
  <ol>{contents.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol>
 </nav>

 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>The Navy's weapons training instruction says that every procedure before the dive has one purpose, to get you to the <Term>roll-in</Term> point, and that what you do between <Term>roll-in</Term> and release decides how accurate the delivery is. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('29')}</a> This module follows that order: the kinds of attack, the geometry of the dive, the numbers you plan, and how to fly them and correct them.</p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to: say when a <Term>laydown</Term>, dive, <Term>loft</Term> or <Term>pop-up</Term> attack is used; draw the bombing triangle and name its angles; read a delivery table and a Z diagram; say whether a release that is steep, shallow, fast, slow, high or low hits long or short, and by about how much; explain what <Term>CCIP</Term> computes and what a fixed <Term id="Depressed sight line">depressed sight</Term> does not; fly an offset <Term>pop-up</Term> through its steps; and explain why the <Term>minimum release altitude</Term> is never broken.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a reference, not a syllabus or a tactic. Worked numbers are the T-45 training jet's practice patterns with practice bombs, from a 2003 revision of its training instruction; combat aircraft, weapons and tactics use their own tables and their service's tactical manuals, which govern. Laser- and coordinate-guided weapons belong to precision targeting, and helicopter gunnery to rotary-wing attack.</p>

 <h2 id="types">Types of delivery</h2>
 <p>The Navy's operational navigation instruction names four types of attack: <a href="#source-p1208">{o('5–6')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong><Term>Laydown</Term></strong>: weapons dropped in level flight, the release point usually set by the weapons system. The weapons use high-drag devices so the attacker can avoid the fragmentation pattern.</li>
  <li><strong>Dive</strong>: the classic delivery, with the aircraft established in a dive of a planned angle down a <Term>run-in line</Term> and the weapon released at a planned altitude and airspeed. The T-45 practice patterns use 10°, 20° and 30° dives. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('21–23')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>Loft</Term></strong>: the weapon is released by computer with the aircraft nose-up, so it is thrown at the target in a ballistic trajectory, which keeps the attacker farther from the detonation and from the target's defenses.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Pop-up</Term></strong>: a low-level run at the target, pulling up hard at a preplanned point to make a dive delivery. In a roll-ahead <Term>pop-up</Term> the heading stays the same; in an angle-off <Term>pop-up</Term> a heading change of 30–60° with a hard climb places the attacker at a roll-in position like a practice pattern's, and the vertical and lateral displacement complicates a defender's firing solution.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>The choice follows the threat and the target. The same instruction says that steep dives need more altitude and so more time climbing from low level, that high altitude avoids small-arms fire and AAA while low altitude avoids SAMs, and that dive angles of 10, 20 or 30° are picked by distance to the target, the threat and the airspace. <a href="#source-p1208">{o('5, 32')}</a> Toss (<Term>loft</Term>) bombing lets an aircraft attack without overflying a heavily defended target and keeps it away from the weapon's blast; the US Air Force called its version the Low Altitude Bombing System (LABS), whose over-the-shoulder variant releases the bomb past the vertical so it is tossed back toward the target. <a href="#source-toss">[5]</a></p>

 <h2 id="triangle">The bombing triangle and the sight</h2>
 <p>A bomb released in a dive starts with the aircraft's velocity, and gravity immediately pulls it down from the aircraft's flight path in an arc. The geometry is the <strong>bombing triangle</strong>: its base is the down-range travel from release to impact, its vertical side is the release altitude, and its hypotenuse is the bomb's <Term>trajectory drop</Term>. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('31')}</a> The angles the pilot works with are: <a href="#source-p1219">{f('29–31')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong><Term>Dive angle</Term></strong>: between the flight path and the ground.</li>
  <li><strong>Angle of attack of the <Term>armament datum line</Term></strong>: between the flight path and a fixed reference line on the aircraft. In the T-45 it shrinks toward zero as the jet accelerates to the 450-knot release airspeed. It is not the angle the <Term>AOA</Term> indicator reads.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Trajectory drop</Term></strong>: the angle from the flight path down to the impact point. The longer the <Term>time of fall</Term>, the longer gravity acts and the larger the drop.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Sight depression angle</Term></strong>: how far the line of sight through the <Term>pipper</Term> is set below the <Term>armament datum line</Term>. It equals the angle of attack plus the <Term>trajectory drop</Term> (plus a couple of mils of parallax), so a pilot who releases at the planned altitude, airspeed and dive angle with the <Term>pipper</Term> on the target hits the target. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('41')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>Sight angles are measured in <Term id="Mil">mils</Term>, 1/6,400 of a circle. A <Term>mil</Term> subtends about one unit at a thousand units, 1 ft at 1,000 ft, and there are roughly 17 <Term id="Mil">mils</Term> in a degree. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('29')}</a> <Term id="Slant range">Slant range</Term> is the straight-line distance from the release point to the impact point, and down-range travel is the same distance over the ground. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('30')}</a></p>

 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="dive" aria-labelledby="dive-title">
  <h3 id="dive-title" class="fig-title">Figure 1 — Dive delivery: fly it wrong and see the hit</h3>
  <p>Pick a T-45 pattern, then add errors. The side view redraws the release; the strip below shows where the bomb hits along the <Term>run-in line</Term>, from the training instruction's table of error sensitivities.</p>
  <fieldset class="inline"><legend>Pattern</legend>
