   <label for="dive-dspeed">Airspeed error <output for="dspeed"></output><input id="dive-dspeed" name="dspeed" type="range" min="-40" max="40" step="10" value="0" /></label>
   <label for="dive-dalt">Release altitude error <output for="dalt"></output><input id="dive-dalt" name="dalt" type="range" min="-400" max="400" step="50" value="0" /></label>
  </div>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-reset>Reset errors</button></div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 1, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 380" role="img" aria-label="Side view of the release point, line of flight and bomb trajectory, with the hit along the run-in line below"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. In a 30° run, 1° of dive angle, 10 kt of airspeed or 100 ft of release altitude each moves the hit about 50 ft: steep, fast or low is long; shallow, slow or high is short.</p>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">The hit comes from the T-45 FTI's table of error sensitivities, for the <Term>pipper</Term> on the aimpoint at release, wings level and correct g, with errors adding or cancelling; the table rounds and applies to small errors. The side view is to scale, with the sight line set for the planned delivery and the hit placed from the same table; the bomb's curve is a no-drag sketch of the triangle's shape, not a ballistic solution, since real bombs slow down and the real sight setting also carries the angle of attack.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="tables">Bombing tables and the Z diagram</h2>
 <p>Bombing tables turn a planned delivery into a sight setting. The T-45's flight manual contains delivery charts for its practice bombs and rockets, from which the pilot finds the sight setting and release attitude for each pattern; target elevation is added to the release altitude to get the release altitude above mean sea level. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('3')}</a> To hit, the pilot decides on a release altitude and airspeed and reads the sight setting for that attack from the table. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('31')}</a> The table also gives the weapon's <Term>time of fall</Term>, which sets the <Term>offset aimpoint</Term>: the wind drifts the bomb after release, so you aim directly into the wind by 1.7 × <Term>time of fall</Term> (s) × wind (kt) ft. With a 12-knot north wind and a 7-second fall, that is about 140 ft north of the target. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('3–4')}</a> Release airspeed is a true airspeed. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('4')}</a></p>
 <p>The numbers for each pattern go on a <strong>Z diagram</strong> on the briefing board or kneeboard card: pattern altitude, airspeed and power, dive angle, checkpoint altitude, initial <Term>pipper</Term> placement, <Term>mil</Term> setting, release altitude, release airspeed, radar altimeter setting and <Term>minimum release altitude</Term>. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('31')}</a> The T-45's three practice patterns: <a href="#source-p1219">{f('21–23, 36')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="T-45 practice weapons patterns" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>T-45 practice bomb patterns, heights above ground <a href="#source-p1219">{f('21–23, 36')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Dive</th><th scope="col">Pattern altitude</th><th scope="col">Pattern speed</th><th scope="col">Release</th><th scope="col">Minimum release</th><th scope="col">Release speed</th><th scope="col">Mil setting</th><th scope="col">Initial sight</th><th scope="col">Weather</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   {rows.map((d) => { const p = PATTERNS[d]; return (
    <tr><th scope="row">{d}°</th><td>{p.patternAlt.toLocaleString('en-US')} ft</td><td>{p.patternKias} KIAS</td><td>{p.release.toLocaleString('en-US')} ft</td><td>{p.minRelease.toLocaleString('en-US')} ft</td><td>{p.releaseKtas} KTAS</td><td>{p.mils}</td><td>{p.initialMils} mils</td><td>{p.weather}</td></tr>
   ); })}
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>The 20° pattern substitutes for the 30° when the ceiling is too low; the 10° pattern simulates high-drag or forward-firing ordnance deliveries. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('22–23')}</a> Practice bombs are filled to simulate general-purpose bomb ballistics: the T-45's <Term record="mk-76">Mk 76</Term> simulates a <Term record="mk-82">Mk 82</Term> 500 lb bomb. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('5')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="pattern">Roll-in, tracking and recovery</h2>
 <p><strong>The <Term>attack cone</Term>.</strong> For a given <Term>dive angle</Term>, all the flight paths to a target form a cone above it. A practice pattern uses a fixed <Term>run-in line</Term>, but in combat you intercept the cone from the most advantageous direction. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('20')}</a> In the T-45 pattern the abeam position, on the cone with the target abeam, is about 2 NM from it, and you stay on the cone from abeam to <Term>roll-in</Term> by keeping the target at 3 or 9 o'clock, much like flying a <Term>TACAN</Term> arc. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('24–25')}</a></p>
 <p><strong><Term>Roll-in</Term>.</strong> Approaching the <Term>run-in line</Term>, set release power and start a 15–17 <Term id="Units">unit</Term> pull toward the target, about 30° of heading before the run-in heading. Roll to put the lift vector slightly below the target and pull the nose down to align with the <Term>run-in line</Term>; up to 10° off it is accepted. Because of the <Term>pendulum effect</Term>, the roll-out starts while the <Term>pipper</Term> is still short of the <Term>run-in line</Term>. Roll wings level and set the target at the initial sight setting. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('25')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Tracking.</strong> In <strong>straight-path tracking</strong>, put the target at the initial sight setting (65 <Term id="Mil">mils</Term> below it for 30° bombs, 70 for 20° and 55 for 10°), note the <Term>dive angle</Term> and hold it, and let the <Term>pipper</Term> track straight up to the aimpoint, arriving just as you reach release altitude. If the <Term>dive angle</Term> is more than 5° off plan, abort and move the <Term>roll-in</Term> point. Make left and right corrections early with quick wing dips, not by tracking in a bank. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('36–37')}</a> Holding the <Term>pipper</Term> on a point with forward stick (curvilinear tracking) steepens the dive and drives g toward zero, so a release then hits long or hangs; the training command does not practice it alone, but it may start a run before straight-path tracking from 1.5 times the release altitude. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('37–38')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Release and recovery.</strong> <Term>Pickle</Term> no lower than the <Term>minimum release altitude</Term>, wings level, in about 1 g. Pause about half a second for the weapon to clear, then pull smoothly to 4 g over 2–3 seconds, wings level. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('25')}</a> In the T-45 the <Term>breakaway cross</Term> appears when a normal 4 g recovery after a normal reaction time would bottom out with 1,000 ft or less of ground clearance; it is not shown in dives shallower than 15°, and it is a warning, not a cue to pull. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('11, 49')}</a> <Term>Target fixation</Term> has flown pilots through the <Term>minimum release altitude</Term>, the <Term>breakaway cross</Term> and the radar altimeter warning into the ground. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('50')}</a></p>
 <p>In the training pattern, "in hot" means you intend to release and the master arm is on, "in cold" that you do not, and "<Term id="WINCHESTER">Winchester</Term>" that all ordnance is expended. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('26, 68')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="errors">Release parameters and errors</h2>
 <p>Every delivery is planned for one <Term>dive angle</Term>, airspeed, release altitude and g, wings level and in balance. Change any of them and the hit moves: <a href="#source-p1219">{f('32–34')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Release altitude.</strong> High gives a longer <Term>time of fall</Term> and a longer distance, so the bomb hits <strong>short</strong>; low hits <strong>long</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>Airspeed.</strong> Fast lowers the angle of attack, bringing the <Term>pipper</Term> short of the impact point, so the hit is <strong>long</strong>; slow hits <strong>short</strong>. Airspeed also changes the <Term>time of fall</Term>.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Dive angle</Term>.</strong> Steep hits <strong>long</strong>, shallow <strong>short</strong>: the steeper the dive, the less gravity bends the trajectory below the line of flight, so the planned <Term>mil</Term> setting is too large.</li>
  <li><strong>G.</strong> The <Term>mil</Term> setting is valid only at the g that holds a straight path: about 0.87 g in a 30° dive, almost 1 g at 10°, 0.5 g at 60°. Too much g gives an early sight picture and a short hit, and shallows the dive; too little hits long and steepens it.</li>
  <li><strong>Bank.</strong> The <Term>pendulum effect</Term> swings the <Term>pipper</Term> opposite to the bank: released in a right bank with the <Term>pipper</Term> on, the hit is right and short.</li>
  <li><strong>Yaw.</strong> Out of balance, the aircraft is not moving where the <Term>pipper</Term> looks: ball out to the right, hit to the right.</li>
  <li><strong>Wind.</strong> Corrected by bank in the pattern, by the <Term>roll-in</Term> point and initial aimpoint during tracking, and by the <Term>offset aimpoint</Term> for the drift after release.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>The training instruction tells students to memorize these error sensitivities. Each column of a row moves the hit by the same amount: <a href="#source-p1219">{f('39')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="T-45 bomb error sensitivities" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>T-45 bomb error sensitivities <a href="#source-p1219">{f('39')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Pattern</th><th scope="col">Dive angle</th><th scope="col">Airspeed</th><th scope="col">Release altitude</th><th scope="col">Hit moves</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   {rows.map((d) => <tr><th scope="row">{d}° bombs</th><td>±1°</td><td>±10 kt</td><td>∓{SENSITIVITY[d].altFt} ft</td><td>±{SENSITIVITY[d].miss} ft</td></tr>)}
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>So in a 30° run, 1° steep or 10 kt fast is 50 ft long, and 100 ft high is 50 ft short. Errors can add, 1° shallow and 10 kt slow being 100 ft short, or cancel, 1° shallow and 10 kt fast needing no correction. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('39, 42')}</a> Rockets are less sensitive to release altitude: 20 ft per 100 ft at 20° and 30°. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('39')}</a> Above all, where the <Term>pipper</Term> is at release is probably the single most important factor: with everything else right and no wind, the bomb hits where the <Term>pipper</Term> was. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('41')}</a></p>

 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="miss" aria-labelledby="miss-title">
  <h3 id="miss-title">Quiz: where does it hit?</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Next run</button></div>
 </section>

 <h2 id="corrections">Correcting in the dive</h2>
 <p>In training you can abort a bad run; in combat you normally get one chance, so you learn to correct. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('39')}</a> The T-45 instruction's corrections for a 30° bomb run: <a href="#source-p1219">{f('40–42')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Steep:</strong> hold the dive and release high, 200 ft high for 2° steep.</li>
  <li><strong>Shallow:</strong> add power, about 4% rpm for 2° shallow, to reach 470 kt and release at the planned altitude.</li>
  <li><strong>20 kt fast:</strong> release 200 ft high, or at the planned altitude with the <Term>pipper</Term> 100 ft short. <strong>20 kt slow:</strong> let the <Term>pipper</Term> drift 100 ft past.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Early sight picture</Term></strong> (the <Term>pipper</Term> reaches the aimpoint above release altitude): hold the dive and release halfway between that altitude and release altitude, at 3,200 ft for a sight picture at 3,400 ft. If it happens every run, you are rolling in too close.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Late sight picture</Term></strong>: cannot be corrected at release. If the <Term>pipper</Term> is more than 150 ft short at release altitude, abort; if less, let it track halfway, under a second, and release. Seen early, add power; do not pull the nose up, which only makes you shallow.</li>
  <li><strong>Deflection:</strong> with bombs there is no last-moment fix; rudder does not move the bomb and a bank gives a false sight picture. Correct early, wings level at release. Forward-firing weapons can be corrected with rudder: for the gun, 1.25 times the error.</li>
 </ul>
 <p>Never release below the <Term>minimum release altitude</Term> to correct anything. It is a safety margin, and it also protects the weapon: a fuze with an arming delay needs a set <Term>time of fall</Term> to arm, so a low release may drop an unarmed bomb, and with live ordnance there is a fragmentation pattern to avoid. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('30, 40, 50')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="sights">Computed and manual sights</h2>
 <p>Everything above is a <strong>manual delivery</strong>: a <Term>depressed sight line</Term>, an "iron sight" set a fixed number of <Term id="Mil">mils</Term> below the line of flight, that is right only when the pilot arrives at the planned parameters. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('67–69')}</a> A computed sight removes most of that work.</p>
 <p><strong><Term>CCIP</Term></strong> (continuously computed impact point) compensates for <Term>dive angle</Term>, airspeed, altitude and target wind and shows on the <Term>HUD</Term> where the weapon would hit if released now. For bombs the T-45 also draws an aiming line above the marker for azimuth steering: put the line over the target, let the marker move up to it and <Term>pickle</Term>. <Term>CCIP</Term> is not magic: it depends on the data entered and a smooth tracking run, releasing near the normal parameters still improves accuracy, and the forecast winds entered may not be the real ones. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('11')}</a> Large stick inputs in the final seconds still spoil hits. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('46')}</a> Because it is hard to fly a consistent <Term>dive angle</Term> and release altitude in a <Term>pop-up</Term> on a target of opportunity, the operational navigation instruction calls <Term>CCIP</Term> the more accurate mode there; on low-level routes the T-45 also flies a simulated level <Term>laydown</Term> by dragging the <Term>CCIP</Term> cross over the target. <a href="#source-p1208">{o('31, 37')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Computed release (CCRP).</strong> The other computed idea lets the system choose the moment of release. The DCS F-14 manual describes the Tomcat's computer target mode as "similar to a CCRP mode in newer aircraft" and its computer pilot mode as similar to <Term>CCIP</Term>. In computer target, the pilot steers to put the target on the bomb fall line on the <Term>HUD</Term> and designates it; the designation is then held on the ground, the pilot flies the velocity vector along the fall line, holds the release button down, and the weapons system releases the bombs when its solution cue reaches the velocity vector. <a href="#source-f14">[6]</a> No military source read for this revision describes a computed release, so this paragraph rests on that sim manual.</p>

 <h2 id="popup">The offset pop-up attack</h2>
 <p>Sometimes a pilot arcs around the <Term>attack cone</Term> before rolling in, but most times you intercept the cone from an ingress heading and pass briefly through it. The T-45 syllabus first practises this in the <Term>pop-up</Term> pattern, ingressing at low altitude and popping up to the cone. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('45')}</a> The <Term>pop-up</Term> is offset to one side so you can keep the target in sight while climbing <a href="#source-p1219">{f('45')}</a>; the operational navigation instruction's angle-off <Term>pop-up</Term> turns 30–60° at the preplanned point, which also complicates a defender's firing solution. <a href="#source-p1208">{o('6')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Ingress and the IP.</strong> On a T-45 low-level, the lead calls "fenced in" at the initial point (IP) for the planned target, or at 10 miles, whichever comes first: air-to-ground mode with bombs and <Term>CCIP</Term> selected, master arm on, and a g and fuel check. <a href="#source-p1208">{o('37')}</a> In the <Term>pop-up</Term> pattern the run-in starts with a level turn inbound at full power (MRT) to cross the <Term>run-in line</Term> with the target about 4 NM off the nose, ingressing level at 700–1,000 ft above the ground; the master arm stays safe until the nose is pointed at the target. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('45')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>The pop, step by step</strong> (T-45 <Term>pop-up</Term> pattern): <a href="#source-p1219">{f('45–46')}</a></p>
 <ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pull-up point.</strong> At about 2.5 NM the target is about 5° down in the <Term>HUD</Term>. Still at MRT, roll 30° of bank away from the pattern and pull to 30° nose up at 17 <Term id="Units">units</Term>, about 30° of heading change.</li>
  <li><strong>Climb.</strong> At 30° nose up, roll wings level in the climb and quickly reacquire the target.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Roll-in</Term>.</strong> Passing 2,000 ft above the ground, roll to put the lift vector on the target and pull down into a 10° dive. Only a few seconds pass between wings level in the climb and the pull-down.</li>
  <li><strong>Track and release.</strong> Roll out in a standard 10° dive over the <Term>run-in line</Term> and track as in the low pattern, with <Term>CCIP</Term>.</li>
  <li><strong>Egress.</strong> Recover straight ahead to 1,000 ft above the ground and call off.</li>
 </ol>

 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="pop" aria-labelledby="pop-title">
  <h3 id="pop-title" class="fig-title">Figure 2 — The pop-up attack in profile and plan</h3>
  <p>Step through the attack or press Play. The profile shows height above the ground, the plan view the offset from the <Term>run-in line</Term>.</p>
  <div class="buttons chips" data-steps></div>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-play>Play</button></div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 2, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 360" role="img" aria-label="Profile and plan view of a pop-up attack: low run-in, offset climb, roll-in, 10 degree dive to release and recovery"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. Run in level at 700–1,000 ft; at about 2.5 NM roll 30° away and pull to 30° nose up; wings level and reacquire; passing 2,000 ft roll in and pull down to a 10° dive on the <Term>run-in line</Term>; track, release and recover straight ahead to 1,000 ft.</p>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Schematic drawn from the T-45 FTI's numbers, not a flown path: the turns, the climb and the dive are placed by hand; the profile's height scale is about two and a half times its distance scale, and the plan's sideways scale twice its distance scale.</p>
 </section>

 <p><strong>A section <Term>pop-up</Term>.</strong> On two-plane road reconnaissance the older operational navigation instruction flies the same attack as a <Term>section</Term>: the lead pops at four miles, when the target is 10–12° below the <Term>HUD</Term> waterline, climbs at full power with about 30° of bank to 30° nose up, then wings level with the target at 10 or 2 o'clock. The <Term>roll-in</Term> comes on reaching the 30° <Term>attack cone</Term>, about two miles from the target: overbank to put the lift vector on the target, pull down at 17 <Term id="Units">units</Term>, and stop with the waterline or velocity vector 4–5° above the target. <a href="#source-p1217">{r('33–35')}</a> An early pop reaches apex altitude before the cone, so you level off and drive in to meet it; a late pop meets the cone below the planned apex and leaves less tracking time. The apex must never exceed the top of the route structure. <a href="#source-p1217">{r('33')}</a> The two jets are deconflicted by altitude: <a href="#source-p1217">{r('33, 36')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Section pop-up altitudes" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Section road-reconnaissance <Term>pop-up</Term>, example altitudes above mean sea level <a href="#source-p1217">{r('33')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Cruise</th><th scope="col">Pull-down</th><th scope="col">Apex</th><th scope="col">Release or recovery start</th><th scope="col">Recovery</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Lead (low)</th><td>3,000 ft</td><td>5,500 ft</td><td>6,000 ft</td><td>4,000 ft</td><td>3,000 ft</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Wingman (high)</th><td>4,000 ft</td><td>6,500 ft</td><td>7,000 ft</td><td>5,000 ft</td><td>No lower than 4,000 ft</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>The lead rolls in first and calls "in low"; the wingman waits for that call, rolls in high and calls "visual" when the lead is back in sight. A wingman with no sight of the lead or the target, or a dive steeper than 35°, goes to high cover instead. With only 1,000 ft to recover from the 30° dive, the recovery is a smooth but aggressive 4–5 g wings-level pull. <a href="#source-p1217">{r('34–36, 38')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="ordnance">Low and high drag, fuzes and fragmentation</h2>
 <p>A low-drag general-purpose bomb (<Term>LDGP</Term>), such as the <Term record="mk-82">Mk 82</Term>, falls with its streamlined shape; fitted with a retarding fin such as the Snakeye, the same bomb can open the fin to fall in a high-drag configuration, used in low-level bombing, and with an in-flight selected mode the pilot chooses which. <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('1-17, 1-24, 1-33')}</a> <Term>Laydown</Term> attacks use such high-drag devices so the attacker can avoid the weapon's fragmentation pattern. <a href="#source-p1208">{o('5')}</a> A <Term>fuze</Term> needs an <Term>arming time</Term> after release before the bomb is armed <a href="#source-navedtra">{nv('1-1')}</a>, and the <Term>minimum release altitude</Term> in the fleet is set by the fragmentation pattern, the time of fall the fuze needs to arm, enemy weapons envelopes and other factors. <a href="#source-p1219">{f('30')}</a> The bombs and rockets themselves, with their fuzes and fins, are in the <a href={ordnance}>ordnance encyclopedia</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="sims">In the sim</h2>
 <p>In DCS, the F-14's computer pilot mode is its <Term>CCIP</Term>, computer target its computed release, and manual mode a <Term id="Depressed sight line">depressed sight</Term> set from engagement tables for speed, <Term>dive angle</Term> and release altitude. <a href="#source-f14">[6]</a> Its computer initial point mode is a computed release from a preset IP: designate a landmark you can see, with the target's range, bearing and height from it entered, and the system steers to the real target. <a href="#source-f14">[6]</a> Beware the word "offset" in the F-4E: there it is a blind-bombing mode in which the crew flies over or radar-locks a known IP and the system releases on north–south and east–west offsets to the target, not the offset <Term>pop-up</Term> of this module. <a href="#source-f4">[7]</a></p>

