 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <details><summary>You release a bomb 200 ft above the planned altitude in a 30° run, everything else right. Long or short, and by how much?</summary><p>Short, by about 100 ft: high releases fall longer, and the T-45 table gives 50 ft per 100 ft.</p></details>
 <details><summary>In a 30° run you are 2° steep approaching release. What do you do?</summary><p>Hold the dive and release about 200 ft high. Never go below the minimum release altitude.</p></details>
 <details><summary>The pipper reaches the aimpoint at 3,400 ft in a run planned for 3,000 ft. When do you release?</summary><p>At about 3,200 ft: split the difference between the early sight picture altitude and the release altitude.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Why can't you fix a bomb's deflection error with rudder at the last moment?</summary><p>The bomb keeps the aircraft's direction of motion, and yaw or bank gives a false sight picture. Correct early and release wings level.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Wind is 10 kt from the west and the time of fall is 6 seconds. Where is the offset aimpoint?</summary><p>About 100 ft west of the target: 1.7 × 6 × 10 = 102 ft, directly into the wind.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Why does a laydown attack use high-drag weapons?</summary><p>So the attacker, releasing in level flight at low altitude, can avoid the weapon's fragmentation pattern.</p></details>
 <details><summary>What is the difference between CCIP and a computed release?</summary><p>CCIP shows where the bomb would hit now and the pilot releases when it is on the target; in a computed release the pilot designates the target and the system computes and makes the release.</p></details>
 <details><summary>In a pop-up, you reach apex altitude before the attack cone. What now?</summary><p>Level off and drive toward the target until you meet the cone, then roll in. Do not climb above the apex or the top of the route.</p></details>

